t $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." 13194139534933 true] [17592186320741 1113 #uuid "38df6533-3590-5853-a74e-af1a3c2be976" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320741 1145 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=9I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320743 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320744 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320745 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320746 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320747 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1041 "selection" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320746 13194139534933 true] [17592186320742 1110 #uuid "1dfe6fcf-ee28-5baa-beef-def0b1219075" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320743 1034 "\\frac{dI}{dt}-3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320743 1111 #uuid "75f927f6-5546-5042-a7a2-7f2fcbed89a4" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320744 1034 "\\frac{dI}{dt}-9I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320744 1111 #uuid "48ac7453-72f7-56c7-8a3e-2fdcbb34cdd1" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320745 1034 "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320745 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320745 1111 #uuid "303ff0a0-bb18-51b4-b5f5-072e815529b7" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320746 1034 "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320746 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320746 1111 #uuid "a2693c51-9dd7-5198-9998-228ef866fb1d" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320747 1034 "\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320747 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320747 1111 #uuid "e2c37afc-a0a4-5da0-8af0-e8c68c2ef928" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320748 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320749 13194139534933 true] [17592186320748 1089 "q-336643" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320748 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534933 true] [17592186320748 1100 "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=3,\\,I(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139534933 true] [17592186320748 1113 #uuid "966610e5-12bf-5626-82d0-fa1440106fe0" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320748 1145 "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{1}{2}$ amperes." 13194139534933 true] [17592186320749 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320750 13194139534933 true] [17592186320749 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320751 13194139534933 true] [17592186320749 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534933 true] [17592186320749 1041 "field-1" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320749 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320750 13194139534933 true] [17592186320749 1110 #uuid "b6f43c4a-9e39-5a07-b318-4ab2942a6e77" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320750 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320750 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320750 1111 #uuid "e3511226-8155-58e2-a084-e9e4cd6ddd0c" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320751 1034 "1" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320751 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320751 1111 #uuid "c4d116bb-35fe-5a58-aabe-a6c545478ee5" 