rac{5}{8}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{5}{8}$ amperes." 13194139534933 true] [17592186320753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320754 13194139534933 true] [17592186320753 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534933 true] [17592186320753 1041 "field-1" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320753 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320754 13194139534933 true] [17592186320753 1110 #uuid "13bfec7e-3d44-5e60-84cf-4f3b9d7df94d" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320754 1034 "\\frac{5}{8}" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320754 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320754 1111 #uuid "1072e537-fd96-5ba7-af06-d2a1ac235901" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320755 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320756 13194139534933 true] [17592186320755 1089 "q-336649" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320755 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534933 true] [17592186320755 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/7e7a2252fdab7a363557a056c58b10f228a13b4aa2029b6b20867a95e5cdde79.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320755 1113 #uuid "040a7edf-5361-5524-9d07-997422bb5a36" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320755 1145 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5=25I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=5.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+25{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n${I}_{p}=\\alpha .$ \nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n \n\n$$\n0+25\\alpha =5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n  Therefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-25t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-25t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{5}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." 13194139534933 true] [17592186320756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320757 13194139534933 true] [17592186320756 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534933 true] [17592186320756 1041 "field-1" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320756 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320757 13194139534933 true] [17592186320756 1110 #uuid "1b510e44-f7db-5f3b-bb10-be17b7b59bae" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320757 1034 "\\frac{4}{5}e^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320757 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320757 1111 #uuid "21a199c7-28ad-56ac-bd99-43440c315095" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320758 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320759 13194139534933 true] [17592186320758 1089 "q-336651" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320758 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534933 true] [17592186320758 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/62dc1d019806908359e3956386f40c1a3ca53d720db7ba0c3122d8eb4ba37c2e.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320758 1113 #uuid "024bf0a5-9232-5172-b017-5423dd2d58c2" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320758 1145 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=16I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A{e}^{-8(0)}+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}={e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." 13194139534933 true] [17592186320759 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320760 13194139534933 true] [17592186320759 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320761 13194139534933 true] [17592186320759 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534933 true] [17592186320759 1041 "field-1" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320759 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320761 13194139534933 true] [17592186320759 1110 #uuid "c5bba320-3b60-538e-9a6a-d823313d1e54" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320760 1034 "e^{-8t}+1" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320760 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320760 1111 #uuid "496db15c-a15f-5d90-b84d-32458421475e" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320761 1034 "e^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534933 true] [17592186320761 1111 #uuid "2c804ffd-1693-5edb-bf5f-d445b32c2dbf" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320763 13194139534933 true] [17592186320762 1089 "q-336653" 13194139534933 true] [17592186320762 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534933 true] [17592186320762 1100 "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.03$ seconds given that the initial current is $3\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nTh